---
title: OpsNow — Authentication & Refresh Token Flow
---
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Browser (SPA)
    participant A as NestJS Auth Module
    participant D as PostgreSQL

    B->>A: POST /auth/login (email + password)
    A->>D: load user row (incl. passwordHash)
    D-->>A: user + passwordHash
    Note over A: Node/Nest verifies Argon2id hash<br/>(argon2.verify) — the database never<br/>performs the verification itself
    A-->>B: short-lived access token (response body)<br/>+ refresh cookie (httpOnly, Secure in prod,<br/>SameSite=Strict, path=/api/v1/auth)

    Note over B: access token held in memory only,<br/>never written to storage

    B->>A: API request, Authorization: Bearer (access token)
    A-->>B: 401 Unauthorized (access token expired)

    B->>A: POST /auth/refresh (cookie sent automatically)
    Note over A: assertTrustedOrigin(): Origin header must<br/>match Host, or the request is rejected —<br/>defense-in-depth alongside SameSite=Strict
    A->>D: atomic transaction:<br/>validate + rotate token
    alt token already rotated (reuse detected)
        A->>D: revoke entire token family
        A-->>B: 401 — session ended
    else token still valid
        A-->>B: new access token + new refresh cookie
    end

    B->>A: POST /auth/logout
    A->>D: revoke the one presented token
    A-->>B: 204 (no-op if already unknown/revoked)

    Note over B,D: Presenting an already-rotated token is treated as suspected theft — revoking the whole family,<br/>not just the reused token. Where supported, navigator.locks serializes refresh attempts across<br/>same-origin tabs, so a legitimate second tab waits and presents the winner's rotated cookie<br/>instead of a stale one. The fallback used when Web Locks is unavailable (jsdom, non-secure<br/>contexts) only deduplicates concurrent refreshes within the current tab — cross-tab<br/>coordination is lost in that case.
